@h1 Emphasis h1@

Wit has exactly two inline marks: @strong bold strong@ and @em italic em@. Two,
on purpose — restraint keeps prose readable.

@@@pre
This word is *bold* and this one is _italic_.
pre@@@

@h2 They wrap a word, not your punctuation h2@

A mark wraps a token, so everyday arithmetic and identifiers stay plain — the
marks don't fire by accident:

@@@pre
The product 3*4*5 and the file_name_here both stay plain text.
pre@@@

@h2 Bold and italic together h2@

Put bold on the outside:

@@@pre
*_both bold and italic_*
pre@@@

The reverse order leaves the inner mark literal, so reach for @code *_…_* code@
when you want both.
